  "source": {
    "image": {
      "path": "/Users/yong/.codex/worktrees/00b1/lemmadeck/.intentfold/tickets/STEMROBIN-181/tmp/d758-source227228-consume-prepare84-85/root/10p/figures/fig-200.png",
      "sha256": "4e2129cea175cc491f118ea3c581db67162cc6807cc0cecc13c23627980c30d5"
    },
    "authoritativeText": [
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s3-n86",
        "text": "知道透镜的曲率半径 $R$，就可以计算暗牛顿环的半径。\n波长为 $\\lambda$ 的光在某些位置相互抵消，这些位置看起来是暗的。\n透镜和玻璃平板之间的空气层，在离接触点同样远的地方厚度相同，所以暗处连成圆环。\n\n这里先讨论透镜和平板在中心真正接触的理想情况。\n如果中心仍有一层小间隙，中心的明暗和各暗环的半径都可能改变，不能直接套用真正接触时的结果。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s3-n86",
        "text": "先把第 $k$ 个暗环的半径记作 $r_k$，把这里的空气层厚度记作 $h_k$。\n图 200 中的直角三角形给出了它们之间的关系。\n根据勾股定理：\n$R^2=r_k^2+(R-h_k)^2$。\n\n展开后得到 $2Rh_k=r_k^2+h_k^2$。\n透镜的曲率半径比波长和 $h_k$ 大得多，薄层又很薄，因此 $h_k\\ll r_k$。\n与环半径的平方相比，厚度的平方很小，可以忽略，于是 $2Rh_k\\approx r_k^2$。\n\n这就得到厚度的近似表达式：\n$h_k\\approx\\frac{r_k^2}{2R}$ (7.6)。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s3-n86",
        "text": "图 200"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s3-n86",
        "text": "比较从空气薄层两侧反射回来的光。\n从下方玻璃平板反射的那列波，在空气层中多走了 $2h_k$，因为它要向下走，再返回。\n它从空气射到玻璃平板并反射时，振动相位还改变了 $\\pi$，也就是振动的起伏反了过来。\n\n从透镜底面反射的那列波，是从玻璃一侧射到空气界面，在这里没有同样的相位反转。\n下方反射引起的相位变化，相当于增加 $\\frac{\\lambda}{2}$ 的程差，并不是光真的又走了一段路。\n这说明本页原注为什么提醒我们：只算空气层中的来回路程，还不能得到用于干涉的全部程差。\n\n在中心真正接触的理想极限，空气层厚度为零，因此没有空气层中的额外来回路程。\n反射造成的相位反转仍使两列反射波相互抵消，所以反射图样的中心是暗的。\n这里的波长是薄层空气中的波长；\n\n像原书一样，计算时把它近似看作真空中的波长。\n要出现暗环，必须把传播和反射带来的相位差一起考虑。\n按第 46 节的干涉最小值条件：\n$2h_k+\\frac{\\lambda}{2}=\\frac{\\lambda}{2}(2k+1)$，\n\n其中 $k=0,1,2\\cdots\\cdots$。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s3-n86",
        "text": "把空气层厚度 $h_k$ 的表达式 (7.6) 代入暗环条件。\n这样就能用波长 $\\lambda$ 和透镜的曲率半径 $R$ 表示暗环半径 $r_k$：\n$r_k=\\sqrt{k\\lambda R}$ (7.7)。\n\n这条关系用于上述反射牛顿环：薄层足够薄，光近乎垂直入射，而且反射相位关系不变。\n零级暗处的半径为零，对应中心暗点，不是一个具有有限半径的圆环。\n用这条半径关系测量波长时，应选中心外的暗环，并保持中心真正接触的条件。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s3-n86",
        "text": "测出某个暗环的半径，再确定这个环的顺序号。\n如果还知道透镜的曲率半径，就可以利用刚得到的关系求出波长。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s3-n86",
        "text": "原书给出的红光测量数值是 $\\lambda_{\\text{红}}=8\\times10^{-5}$ 厘米，紫光测量数值是 $\\lambda_{\\text{紫}}=4\\times10^{-5}$ 厘米。\n原书用这些数值说明光谱中其他颜色的波长处于它们之间（见彩图 V）。\n这是原书给出的数值，不应把它们当成红光和紫光的精确分界。\n\n各种可见光的波长都很短。\n原书用海波作比较：海波的平均波长可以有几米。\n\n设想把这样的海波放大到能从美洲海岸跨过大西洋到欧洲海岸，再把光波按同样的比例放大。\n放大后的光波，其波长仍只稍稍超过这本书一页的宽度。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s3-n86",
        "text": "干涉现象既说明光具有波动性，也让我们能够测量光的波长。\n声音的音调用频率区分；\n单色光的颜色也与频率有关，在同一种介质中可以用波长来区分。\n\n原书给出的关系 $\\lambda\\nu=c$ 中，波长和频率相乘得到真空中的光速。\n因此，这里的波长指真空中的波长；\n改变光所处的介质时，不能仍把介质中的波长和真空光速直接代入这条关系。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s3-n86",
        "text": "颜色是眼睛接收光以后产生的感觉；\n光本身有不同的波长。\n原书用约 $10^{-6}$ 厘米的波长差，说明眼睛可以分辨很小的差别。\n\n这个数值是原书的估计，不表示眼睛在任何颜色和观察条件下都有相同的分辨能力。\n原书还把大多数动物的视觉说成只能看到黑白。\n这项概括并不准确：不同动物辨认颜色的能力并不相同。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s3-n86",
        "text": "光进入另一种介质时，波长会改变。\n可以在透镜和玻璃平板之间原来装空气的薄层中，填入水或折射率为 $n$ 的透明液体。\n\n对于仍保持前面反射相位关系的这类液体，干涉环的半径会减小。\n比较时保持光源和透镜不变，这样就能观察介质对环半径的影响。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s3-n86",
        "text": "为什么填入液体后，环会缩小？\n光从真空进入本课讨论的透明介质时，折射率为 $n$，表示光速比真空中的光速小相应的倍数。\n在介质中，速度、波长和频率仍满足 $v=\\lambda\\nu$。\n\n光进入介质时频率保持不变，所以波长缩短为真空中波长的 $\\frac{1}{n}$。\n因此，前面的环半径关系要使用薄层介质中的波长，不能继续使用真空中的波长。\n这里说波长改变了 $n$ 倍，是指波长缩短，不是增大；\n\n频率不变，光也不会仅仅因为进入介质就变成另一种颜色。\n在反射相位关系保持不变的条件下，较短的波长对应较小的暗环半径。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s3-n86",
        "text": "Knowing the lens's radius of curvature $R$ lets us calculate the radii of the dark Newton rings.\nLight of wavelength $\\lambda$ cancels at certain places, making those places dark.\n\nThe air gap between the lens and the flat glass plate has the same thickness all around each circle centered on their contact point.\nThat is why the dark places form rings.\n\nWe first consider the ideal case in which the lens and plate truly touch at the center.\nIf a small gap remains there, the central brightness and dark-ring radii may change, so the true-contact results cannot be applied directly."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s3-n86",
        "text": "For the dark ring with order $k$, call its radius $r_k$ and the air gap's thickness there $h_k$.\nThe right triangle in Figure 200 connects these quantities.\n\nThe Pythagorean theorem gives:\n$R^2=r_k^2+(R-h_k)^2$.\nExpanding gives $2Rh_k=r_k^2+h_k^2$.\n\nThe lens's radius of curvature is much larger than the wavelength and $h_k$.\nThe gap is very thin, so $h_k\\ll r_k$.\n\nThe squared thickness is tiny compared with the squared ring radius, so we neglect it and get $2Rh_k\\approx r_k^2$.\nThe approximate thickness is:\n$h_k\\approx\\frac{r_k^2}{2R}$ (7.6)."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s3-n86",
        "text": "Figure 200"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s3-n86",
        "text": "Compare the light reflected from the opposite sides of the thin air gap.\nThe wave reflected from the glass plate below travels an extra distance $2h_k$ through the air, going down and back up.\n\nWhen it reflects from air onto glass, its phase also changes by $\\pi$: its oscillation is reversed.\nThe wave reflected at the lens's underside meets the air boundary from inside the glass.\n\nIt does not have the same reflection reversal there.\nThe lower reflection's phase change is equivalent to an extra path difference $\\frac{\\lambda}{2}$, not extra distance actually traveled.\n\nThis explains the source footnote's warning: travel through the gap alone does not give the full path difference used for interference.\nHere the wavelength is the wavelength in the air gap, approximated as the vacuum wavelength as in the source calculation.\n\nIn the ideal limit of true contact at the center, the air gap has zero thickness, so there is no extra round trip through air.\nThe phase reversal on reflection still makes the reflected waves cancel, giving a dark center in the reflected pattern.\n\nA dark ring depends on the phase difference from both travel and reflection.\nUsing the interference-minimum condition from Section 46:\n\n$2h_k+\\frac{\\lambda}{2}=\\frac{\\lambda}{2}(2k+1)$,\nwhere $k=0,1,2\\cdots\\cdots$."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s3-n86",
        "text": "Substitute the expression for the air gap's thickness $h_k$ from (7.6) into the dark-ring condition.\nThe wavelength $\\lambda$ and the lens's radius of curvature $R$ then determine the dark ring's radius $r_k$:\n$r_k=\\sqrt{k\\lambda R}$ (7.7).\n\nThis relation describes the reflected Newton rings above: a very thin gap, nearly perpendicular light, and the same reflection-phase relationship.\nThe zero-order dark region has zero radius: it is the central dark spot, not a ring of finite radius.\nTo measure wavelength using this radius relation, choose a dark ring outside the center and maintain true contact at the center."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s3-n86",
        "text": "Measure a dark ring's radius and identify its order.\nIf you also know the lens's radius of curvature, the relation just found lets you determine the wavelength."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s3-n86",
        "text": "The source reports $\\lambda_{\\text{红}}=8\\times10^{-5}$ centimeters for red light and $\\lambda_{\\text{紫}}=4\\times10^{-5}$ centimeters for violet light.\nIt uses these values to describe the wavelengths of the other spectral colors as lying between them (see color plate V; source citation: 彩图 V).\n\nThese are the source's reported values, not precise boundaries defining red and violet.\nAll visible-light wavelengths are very short.\n\nThe source compares them with sea waves whose average wavelength is a few meters.\nImagine enlarging such a sea wave until it stretches across the Atlantic from the American coast to the European coast.\nEnlarge a light wave by the same factor, and its wavelength would still be only a little wider than a page of this book."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s3-n86",
        "text": "Interference shows that light behaves as a wave and gives us a way to measure its wavelength.\nFrequency distinguishes the pitch of a sound.\n\nThe color of monochromatic light is also related to frequency;\nwithin the same medium, wavelength can distinguish its color.\n\nIn the source's relation $\\lambda\\nu=c$, wavelength times frequency gives the speed of light in vacuum.\nThe wavelength in this relation is therefore the vacuum wavelength.\nAfter changing the medium, we must not substitute its wavelength while still using the vacuum speed."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s3-n86",
        "text": "Color is a sensation produced when our eyes receive light;\nlight itself has different wavelengths.\n\nThe source gives about $10^{-6}$ centimeters as a small wavelength difference the eye can distinguish.\nThat is the source's estimate, not the same limit for every color and viewing condition.\n\nThe source also claims that most animals see only black and white.\nThat is not an accurate general rule: animals differ in their ability to distinguish colors."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s3-n86",
        "text": "Light's wavelength changes when it enters another medium.\nFill the gap between the lens and glass plate with water or a transparent liquid of refractive index $n$ instead of air.\n\nFor such liquids that keep the reflection-phase relationship described above, the interference rings become smaller.\nKeep the light source and lens unchanged when comparing the rings, so the change shows the medium's effect."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s3-n86",
        "text": "Why do the rings shrink after adding the liquid?\nWhen light enters the transparent medium considered here from vacuum, refractive index $n$ describes the factor by which its speed decreases.\n\nSpeed, wavelength, and frequency in the medium still satisfy $v=\\lambda\\nu$.\nThe frequency stays unchanged, so the wavelength becomes $\\frac{1}{n}$ of its vacuum value.\n\nThe ring-radius relation must therefore use the wavelength in the gap's medium, not the vacuum wavelength.\nThe source's statement that wavelength changes by a factor of $n$ means that it becomes shorter, not longer.\n\nThe frequency stays unchanged, so entering another medium alone does not change the light into another color.\nWith the reflection-phase relationship unchanged, a shorter wavelength gives smaller dark rings."
      }
    ],
